      {
        type: "application",
        title: "What the Campaign Asks of Us",
        content: "The Poor People's Campaign is the part of King's life that is hardest to commemorate, because to honor it requires holding economic positions that remain politically contested. The Martin Luther King Jr. National Holiday celebrates the dreamer. The Lincoln Memorial steps now feature an inscribed marker noting where 'I Have a Dream' was delivered. But there is no federal monument to the Mule Train that traveled from Marks, Mississippi. The Resurrection City eviction is not in most textbooks. The reason, as Brandeis historian Brandon Terry has argued, is that 'sanitization' of King has erased his economic radicalism in favor of his moral rhetoric. Your application this week has three parts. ONE, locate one quote from King in 1967 or 1968 that you have never seen on a poster or in a corporate Martin Luther King Jr. Day email. Sources: the 1967 'Beyond Vietnam' speech, the 1968 'I See the Promised Land' speech, the August 1967 SCLC Presidential Address 'Where Do We Go from Here?', or the 1967 Massey Lectures broadcast on Canadian radio. TWO, ask whether any of King's specific economic demands have been met since 1968. Has there been a guaranteed annual income? (No.) Has the share of national wealth held by the bottom 50% grown? (No, it has fallen.) Has the Black-white wealth gap closed? (It has widened in absolute dollars.) THREE, consider the Reverend William Barber II's revived Poor People's Campaign, launched in 2018 on the fiftieth anniversary of Resurrection City. Barber explicitly framed the new campaign as continuing King's unfinished work. Read his core demands and compare them to King's 1968 demands. The continuity is the point. The campaign is not over.",
        visual: {
          type: "diagram",
          svg: `<svg viewBox="0 0 1100 1100" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><rect width="1100" height="1100" fill="#1a1a2e"/><text x="550" y="60" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffd700" font-size="30" font-weight="bold">The Unfinished Campaign</text><rect x="80" y="120" width="940" height="220" fill="#6366f1" opacity="0.85" rx="15"/><text x="550" y="160" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffd700" font-size="22" font-weight="bold">PART ONE: Find a sanitized-out quote</text><line x1="100" y1="180" x2="1000" y2="180" stroke="#ffffff" stroke-width="2"/><text x="100" y="215" fill="#ffffff" font-size="16">Sources you will not find on a corporate MLK Day email:</text><text x="120" y="245" fill="#ffffff" font-size="15">- "Beyond Vietnam" - April 4, 1967 (Riverside Church)</text><text x="120" y="275" fill="#ffffff" font-size="15">- "I See the Promised Land" - April 3, 1968 (Memphis)</text><text x="120" y="305" fill="#ffffff" font-size="15">- "Where Do We Go from Here?" - August 1967 (SCLC)</text><text x="120" y="335" fill="#ffffff" font-size="15">- The Massey Lectures - 1967 (CBC Radio Canada)</text><rect x="80" y="360" width="940" height="220" fill="#10b981" opacity="0.85" rx="15"/><text x="550" y="400" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffd700" font-size="22" font-weight="bold">PART TWO: Audit King's 1968 demands today</text><line x1="100" y1="420" x2="1000" y2="420" stroke="#ffffff" stroke-width="2"/><text x="100" y="460" fill="#ffffff" font-size="16">Has there been a guaranteed annual income? NO</text><text x="100" y="495" fill="#ffffff" font-size="16">Has bottom 50% share of wealth grown? NO (it has fallen)</text><text x="100" y="530" fill="#ffffff" font-size="16">Has the Black-white wealth gap closed? NO (it has widened)</text><text x="100" y="565" fill="#ffffff" font-size="16">Has full employment been achieved? NO</text><rect x="80" y="600" width="940" height="240" fill="#f59e0b" opacity="0.85" rx="15"/><text x="550" y="640" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1a1a2e" font-size="22" font-weight="bold">PART THREE: The Reverend Barber Revival</text><line x1="100" y1="660" x2="1000" y2="660" stroke="#1a1a2e" stroke-width="2"/><text x="100" y="700" fill="#1a1a2e" font-size="16">2018: Rev. William Barber II launches new Poor People's Campaign</text><text x="100" y="735" fill="#1a1a2e" font-size="16">Fiftieth anniversary of Resurrection City</text><text x="100" y="770" fill="#1a1a2e" font-size="16">Explicit continuation of King's economic agenda</text><text x="100" y="805" fill="#1a1a2e" font-size="16">Compare Barber's demands to King's 1968 demands. The overlap is the point.</text><rect x="80" y="860" width="940" height="180" fill="#ffd700" opacity="0.9" rx="15"/><text x="550" y="900" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1a1a2e" font-size="22" font-weight="bold">The sanitization thesis (Brandon Terry)</text><text x="100" y="945" fill="#1a1a2e" font-size="17">"What gets remembered is the dreamer.</text><text x="100" y="975" fill="#1a1a2e" font-size="17">What gets forgotten is the economic radical.</text><text x="100" y="1005" fill="#1a1a2e" font-size="17">The forgetting is not accidental. It is doing political work."</text></svg>`,
          caption: "Honoring the Poor People's Campaign requires economic conviction"
        }
      }
