      {
        type: "application",
        title: "What Surveillance Teaches About Power",
        content: "There are three durable lessons in the FBI's campaign against King that apply to civic life today. ONE: institutional surveillance does not require villainy at the top to produce monstrous outcomes. Hoover was an obsessive racist, but the program required ordinary FBI agents (some of whom were sympathetic to civil rights) to type the memos, mail the letters, and rotate the wiretap shifts. Bureaucratic momentum sustained it. The lesson is not 'one bad apple.' The lesson is that processes generate atrocities when nobody refuses. TWO: the public was unable to act on the surveillance because they did not know about it. From 1962 to 1971, the press did not report COINTELPRO. Several journalists had partial knowledge but chose not to publish, in part because they accepted FBI framing that King was 'compromised.' The break came not from journalism but from a citizens' burglary: on March 8, 1971, eight peace activists calling themselves the Citizens' Commission to Investigate the FBI broke into the Media, Pennsylvania, FBI office during the Muhammad Ali-Joe Frazier fight (chosen because Americans would be distracted) and stole over a thousand files. They mailed copies to the Washington Post, New York Times, and Los Angeles Times. Only the Post initially published. Without that burglary, COINTELPRO might have continued for years. THREE: the Church Committee (1975-76), chaired by Senator Frank Church of Idaho, produced fourteen volumes of investigation that remain the foundational document on U.S. intelligence overreach. Your application this week is to do two things. First, read the Church Committee's Book III summary, especially pages 79-184 on COINTELPRO. The PDF is free online at intelligence.senate.gov. Second, ask: what surveillance program operating today might require a future Church Committee? The FBI's post-9/11 NSL system? Cell-site simulators? Predictive policing algorithms? Section 702 of FISA? Each of these has King-like implications. The pattern is older than the bureau, and it is not finished.",
        visual: {
          type: "diagram",
          svg: `<svg viewBox="0 0 1100 1100" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><rect width="1100" height="1100" fill="#1a1a2e"/><text x="550" y="60" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffd700" font-size="30" font-weight="bold">Three Lessons of Surveillance</text><rect x="80" y="120" width="940" height="220" fill="#6366f1" opacity="0.85" rx="15"/><text x="550" y="160" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffd700" font-size="22" font-weight="bold">LESSON 1: Bureaucracy Generates Atrocity</text><line x1="100" y1="180" x2="1000" y2="180" stroke="#ffffff" stroke-width="2"/><text x="100" y="215" fill="#ffffff" font-size="16">Hoover was racist, but he could not mail letters alone.</text><text x="100" y="245" fill="#ffffff" font-size="16">Ordinary agents typed memos, rotated wiretaps, mailed packages.</text><text x="100" y="275" fill="#ffffff" font-size="16">Some were sympathetic to civil rights. They did the work anyway.</text><text x="100" y="310" fill="#ffd700" font-size="16">The lesson is not "bad apples." It is that PROCESSES need REFUSERS.</text><rect x="80" y="360" width="940" height="240" fill="#10b981" opacity="0.85" rx="15"/><text x="550" y="400" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffd700" font-size="22" font-weight="bold">LESSON 2: Secrecy Disables Citizens</text><line x1="100" y1="420" x2="1000" y2="420" stroke="#ffffff" stroke-width="2"/><text x="100" y="455" fill="#ffffff" font-size="16">1962-71: press did not report COINTELPRO</text><text x="100" y="485" fill="#ffffff" font-size="16">Several reporters knew. Some accepted FBI framing of King.</text><text x="100" y="515" fill="#ffffff" font-size="16">March 8, 1971: Citizens' Commission burgles Media, PA office</text><text x="100" y="545" fill="#ffffff" font-size="16">Chose night of Ali-Frazier fight for distraction</text><text x="100" y="575" fill="#ffffff" font-size="16">Files mailed to Post, Times, LA Times. Only Post published.</text><rect x="80" y="620" width="940" height="220" fill="#f59e0b" opacity="0.85" rx="15"/><text x="550" y="660" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1a1a2e" font-size="22" font-weight="bold">LESSON 3: Public Investigation Matters</text><line x1="100" y1="680" x2="1000" y2="680" stroke="#1a1a2e" stroke-width="2"/><text x="100" y="715" fill="#1a1a2e" font-size="16">1975-76: Church Committee under Sen. Frank Church (D-Idaho)</text><text x="100" y="745" fill="#1a1a2e" font-size="16">Produced 14 volumes documenting CIA, FBI, NSA abuses</text><text x="100" y="775" fill="#1a1a2e" font-size="16">Book III, pp. 79-184: COINTELPRO in detail</text><text x="100" y="805" fill="#1a1a2e" font-size="16">Free PDF at intelligence.senate.gov</text><rect x="80" y="860" width="940" height="180" fill="#ef4444" opacity="0.85" rx="15"/><text x="550" y="900" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffffff" font-size="22" font-weight="bold">Where to look today</text><text x="100" y="940" fill="#ffffff" font-size="17">- FBI post-9/11 National Security Letter program</text><text x="100" y="970" fill="#ffffff" font-size="17">- Cell-site simulators ("StingRays") used by local police</text><text x="100" y="1000" fill="#ffffff" font-size="17">- Predictive policing algorithms (PredPol, Palantir)</text><text x="100" y="1030" fill="#ffffff" font-size="17">- Section 702 of FISA: warrantless backdoor searches</text></svg>`,
          caption: "The pattern is older than the FBI and is not finished"
        }
      }
