          svg: `<svg viewBox="0 0 1100 1100" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><rect width="1100" height="1100" fill="#1a1a2e"/><text x="550" y="60" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffd700" font-size="30" font-weight="bold">Reading Coretta as a Political Thinker</text><rect x="80" y="120" width="940" height="200" fill="#6366f1" opacity="0.85" rx="15"/><text x="550" y="160" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffd700" font-size="22" font-weight="bold">PART 1: Read her memoir as autobiography</text><line x1="100" y1="180" x2="1000" y2="180" stroke="#ffffff" stroke-width="2"/><text x="100" y="220" fill="#ffffff" font-size="17">My Life with Martin Luther King, Jr. (1969, revised 1993)</text><text x="100" y="255" fill="#ffffff" font-size="17">Chapters 3-4: her activism before Martin</text><text x="100" y="290" fill="#ffffff" font-size="17">Chapter 21: her response to his death</text><rect x="80" y="340" width="940" height="280" fill="#10b981" opacity="0.85" rx="15"/><text x="550" y="380" text-anchor="middle" fill="#ffd700" font-size="22" font-weight="bold">PART 2: Three positions independent of Martin</text><line x1="100" y1="400" x2="1000" y2="400" stroke="#ffffff" stroke-width="2"/><text x="100" y="440" fill="#ffffff" font-size="17">A. Anti-apartheid leadership (1980s)</text><text x="120" y="470" fill="#ffffff" font-size="15">King Center became major U.S. organizing hub</text><text x="100" y="510" fill="#ffffff" font-size="17">B. LGBT rights defense (1998)</text><text x="120" y="540" fill="#ffffff" font-size="15">When most Black clergy were silent or opposed</text><text x="100" y="580" fill="#ffffff" font-size="17">C. Opposition to death penalty (1990s-2000s)</text><text x="120" y="610" fill="#ffffff" font-size="15">Martin never spoke publicly against capital punishment</text><rect x="80" y="640" width="940" height="220" fill="#f59e0b" opacity="0.85" rx="15"/><text x="550" y="680" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1a1a2e" font-size="22" font-weight="bold">PART 3: Theoharis's diagnostic question</text><line x1="100" y1="700" x2="1000" y2="700" stroke="#1a1a2e" stroke-width="2"/><text x="100" y="740" fill="#1a1a2e" font-size="17">Why is Coretta rarely cited as an intellectual force?</text><text x="100" y="775" fill="#1a1a2e" font-size="17">"Widow of Dr. King" framing has done political work:</text><text x="100" y="810" fill="#1a1a2e" font-size="17">Marginalized her activism. Made her unreadable as a serious actor.</text><text x="100" y="845" fill="#1a1a2e" font-size="17">Allowed politicians to praise Martin while ignoring Coretta's stands.</text><rect x="80" y="880" width="940" height="180" fill="#ffd700" opacity="0.9" rx="15"/><text x="550" y="920" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1a1a2e" font-size="22" font-weight="bold">The thought experiment</text><text x="100" y="960" fill="#1a1a2e" font-size="17">Imagine Coretta had never married Martin.</text><text x="100" y="990" fill="#1a1a2e" font-size="17">Her résumé alone is a major American political career.</text><text x="100" y="1020" fill="#1a1a2e" font-size="17">When you say "King" this week, mean both of them.</text></svg>`,
